      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. Semantically, a stride should be related to the width, not the height. Should we use `kWidth` instead of `kHeight` in the initializers?\n2. I would remove `kWidth - 1`, which is an invalid stride on its own.\n3. These strides are wrong by a factor of two for high bit depths. This is quite confusing.",
      "revId": "0ed903ee59cfc1b7dbab950f4e0d1445b14148b4",
      "serverId": "e5514cf8-2d6e-3e29-adb4-24cd6dde4bf0"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "16aef0c5_5878c5b6",
        "filename": "test/encode_api_test.cc",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 210,
      "author": {
        "id": 5020
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-23T23:31:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e 1. Semantically, a stride should be related to the width, not the height. Should we use `kWidth` instead of `kHeight` in the initializers?\n\nYou\u0027re right, I mixed the two after reworking the test and it\u0027s only correct given width and height are the same here.\n\n\u003e 2. I would remove `kWidth - 1`, which is an invalid stride on its own.\n\nIt\u0027s useful to test the value regardless of the size of the allocation. I\u0027m only using values that won\u0027t overrun the buffer.\n\n\u003e 3. These strides are wrong by a factor of two for high bit depths. This is quite confusing.\n\nNote the comment for the allocation. The width and height don\u0027t particularly matter, just that the values fit.",
      "parentUuid": "9d6004df_bf126010",
      "revId": "0ed903ee59cfc1b7dbab950f4e0d1445b14148b4",
      "serverId": "e5514cf8-2d6e-3e29-adb4-24cd6dde4bf0"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f341c63_14fcaba0",
        "filename": "test/encode_api_test.cc",
